INT. COURTHOUSE FREIGHT ELEVATOR - STUCK BETWEEN FLOORS - NIGHT

A steel box with a scarred wooden floor. A work light flickers. A stenciled sign: MAX LOAD 4000 LBS.

ROSALIND PETTIGREW, 50s, prosecutor, grips a banker's box. DEMETRIUS OKONKWO-HALE, 40s, defense counsel, leans on the back wall with a coffee cup.

The elevator hangs still. A cable creaks overhead.

ROSALIND
Twelve minutes.

DEMETRIUS
It's been eleven.

ROSALIND
Then you can count. Good. Count how long my offer stands.

DEMETRIUS
Your offer is manslaughter and eight years.

ROSALIND
It's a gift.

DEMETRIUS
It's a coin flip with the coin removed.

She presses the alarm button. Nothing. She presses it again.

ROSALIND
Your client lied to the detective.

DEMETRIUS
Your detective led him.

ROSALIND
Your client said he was at the depot.

DEMETRIUS
He was at the depot.

ROSALIND
Until nine.

DEMETRIUS
Until nine fifteen.

ROSALIND
The body was found at nine twenty.

DEMETRIUS
The depot is eleven minutes from the creek.

ROSALIND
Then he ran.

DEMETRIUS
In dress shoes. Carrying a toolbox.

Rosalind sets the banker's box down. Opens it. Pulls a photograph. Holds it out without looking at him.

ROSALIND
Look at it.

DEMETRIUS
I've seen it.

ROSALIND
Look at it again.

He takes the photograph. He studies it. His jaw works.

DEMETRIUS
The shoe prints don't match.

ROSALIND
The prints are smudged.

DEMETRIUS
They're smudged because your tech walked through them.

ROSALIND
That is a very convenient theory.

DEMETRIUS
It's a photograph.

The elevator lurches. Both of them grab the rail. The coffee spills across the floor. Both stare at the puddle as it spreads toward the box.

Rosalind snatches the box up. Demetrius shoves his foot against the puddle, blocking it.

DEMETRIUS (CONT'D)
That box is evidence.

ROSALIND
That box is mine.

DEMETRIUS
Then keep it dry.

They both stop moving. The cable stops creaking. Silence.

ROSALIND
Was that the brake?

DEMETRIUS
I don't know.

ROSALIND
Don't say "I don't know" to me.

DEMETRIUS
I'm not your witness.

ROSALIND
You're the one who wanted to take the freight elevator.

DEMETRIUS
You pushed the button.

ROSALIND
I was pointing at it.

DEMETRIUS
You pushed it.

Rosalind sits down on the box. Straightens her skirt. Looks up at the ceiling vent, then at him.

ROSALIND
Six years.

DEMETRIUS
Four.

ROSALIND
Five, and he pleads to the lesser count.

DEMETRIUS
No.

ROSALIND
No?

DEMETRIUS
He didn't do it.

ROSALIND
Mr. Okonkwo-Hale, I have a thirty-year record that says everyone says that.

DEMETRIUS
And your record never once says someone was right?

Rosalind stands. They are close in the small space. Neither backs away.

ROSALIND
You think I want to be right?

DEMETRIUS
I think you want to be done.

ROSALIND
That's the same thing.

DEMETRIUS
It isn't.

The light flickers off. Total dark. A beat. Two beats. It flickers on.

They are exactly where they were. Neither has moved.

ROSALIND
Four years. Plea to the lesser. I'll sign it in this box.

DEMETRIUS
I'll need to hear it from you after we're out.

ROSALIND
You'll hear it.

DEMETRIUS
I'll need it in writing.

ROSALIND
Do you have a pen?

He pats his pockets. She pats hers. They both check their jackets, then each other's eyes.

DEMETRIUS
No.

ROSALIND
Of course not.

The elevator groans. Begins, slowly, to rise. They both look up. Neither speaks. The floor numbers light, one by one, over the door.
